    <div class=3D"dds__container" style=3D"max-width:1920px">
        <div class=3D"dds__justify-content-center">
            <h1 class=3D"dds__mt-4 dds__mb-4">Download and Create Ubuntu Re=
covery Image</h1>
           =20

            <div class=3D"dds__row">
                <div class=3D"dds__col--lg-9 dds__col--md-9">
                    <div role=3D"region" id=3D"Description" class=3D"dds__a=
ccordion dds__mt-5 dds__mb-5" data-dds=3D"accordionhtml" data-independent=
=3D"true" data-sr-expand-item=3D"item expanded." data-sr-collapse-item=3D"i=
tem collapsed.">
                        Installing Ubuntu on your Dell computer gives you a=
ccess to a free, secure, and customizable operating system. Dell provides t=
ested Ubuntu ISO images specifically designed for supported Dell computers,=
 ensuring optimal compatibility and performance. Having access to the right=
 Ubuntu ISO file and knowing how to create a proper recovery key can save y=
ou a lot of time.<p></p><p>In this comprehensive guide, we'll walk you thro=
ugh downloading the official Ubuntu ISO from Dell, creating a bootable USB =
drive, and installing Ubuntu on your Dell system. We'll cover two common sc=
enarios: creating a recovery media using a working computer with Ubuntu or =
Windows.</p><p><img src=3D"https://i.dell.com/is/image/DellContent/content/=
dam/ss2/product-images/esupport/esupport-pages/ubuntu-desktop-rounded-corne=
rs.png" width=3D"600" alt=3D"" class=3D"dds__mw-100" role=3D"presentation" =
aria-hidden=3D"true"></p><hr><h2>What You'll Need</h2><ul><li>Service Tag o=
f the Dell computer that was shipped with Ubuntu</li><li>Access to a workin=
g computer with Ubuntu or Windows</li><li>A blank USB drive (8GB minimum re=
commended)</li><li>A reliable internet connection for downloading the ISO f=
ile</li><li>Administrative access on the working computer</li></ul><hr><h2>=
Downloading Ubuntu ISO File from Dell Drivers &amp; Downloads</h2><p>Dell p=
rovides tested Ubuntu images that work optimally with your Dell hardware. B=
efore creating your recovery media, you'll need to download the Ubuntu ISO =
file for your Dell computer. Here's how to get the right ISO file for your =
system:</p><h3>Step 1: Navigating to Dell Drivers &amp; Downloads Website</=
h3><ol><li>Open a web browser and go to Dell <a href=3D"https://www.dell.co=
m/support/home/en-us?app=3Ddrivers" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noopener" clas=
s=3D"dds__link--standalone">Drivers &amp; Downloads</a>.</li><li>Enter your=
 <strong>Service Tag</strong>, <strong>Express Service Code</strong>, or <s=
trong>model number</strong> in the search field and click <strong>Search</s=
trong>.</li><li>Alternatively, click on <strong>Browse all products</strong=
>, select your specific model from the catalog.</li></ol><h3>Step 2: Downlo=
ad the Ubuntu ISO</h3><ol><li>In the <strong>Operating system</strong> drop=
down, select <strong>Ubuntu</strong>.</li><li>Look for the <strong>Ubuntu R=
ecovery Image</strong> option.</li><li>Click <strong>Download</strong> and =
save the ISO file to your Downloads folder.</li></ol><p><strong>Note</stron=
g>: If you don't see Ubuntu Recovery Image for your model, your Dell comput=
er may not have tested Ubuntu support.</p><hr><h2>Creating a Bootable USB D=
rive in Ubuntu</h2><p>If you're creating a bootable USB drive on your Ubunt=
u system or using another working computer with Ubuntu, use the built-in St=
artup Disk Creator to make your bootable USB recovery media.</p><h3>Using S=
tartup Disk Creator</h3><ol><li>Connect your USB drive into an available US=
B port.</li><li>Open the <strong>Activities</strong> overview by pressing t=
he <strong>Super</strong> key.</li><li>Type <strong>Startup Disk Creator</s=
trong> and click to open it.</li><li>In the <strong>Source disc image</stro=
ng> section:<ol style=3D"list-style-type: lower-alpha;"><li>Click <strong>O=
ther</strong> to browse for your downloaded Ubuntu ISO file.</li><li>Naviga=
te to your <strong>Downloads</strong> folder and select the Ubuntu ISO</li>=
</ol></li><li>In the <strong>Disk to use</strong> section:<ol style=3D"list=
-style-type: lower-alpha;"><li>Select your USB drive from the list.<br><str=
ong>Warning</strong>: This will erase all data on the USB drive.</li></ol><=
/li><li>Click <strong>Make Startup Disk</strong>.</li><li>Enter your passwo=
rd when prompted.</li><li>Wait for the process to complete (usually 5-10 mi=
nutes).</li><li>Click <strong>Quit</strong> when finished.</li></ol><p>Your=
 bootable Ubuntu USB drive is now ready for installation. Next, let's <a hr=
ef=3D"https://www.dell.com/support/contents/en-us/article/product-support/s=
elf-support-knowledgebase/software-and-downloads/how-to-download-install-ub=
untu-linux-recovery-image#h_2841198707771749134489971" class=3D"dds__link--=
standalone">install Ubuntu</a>.</p><p><img src=3D"https://i.dell.com/is/ima=
ge/DellContent/content/dam/ss2/product-images/esupport/esupport-pages/ubunt=
u-startup-disk-creator.png" width=3D"600" alt=3D"" class=3D"dds__mw-100" ro=
le=3D"presentation" aria-hidden=3D"true"></p><hr><h2>Creating a Bootable US=
B Drive in Windows</h2><p>If you're currently using Windows, you can use th=
e Dell OS Recovery Tool to create your bootable Ubuntu USB drive.</p><h3>Us=
ing Dell OS Recovery Tool</h3><ol><li>Download <a href=3D"https://www.dell.=
com/support/home/en-us/drivers/osiso/recoverytool" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D=
"noopener" class=3D"dds__link--standalone">Dell OS Recovery Tool</a>.</li><=
li>Install and run <strong>Dell OS Recovery Tool</strong> as administrator.=
</li><li>Connect your USB drive.</li><li>Click <strong>SWITCH TO ADVANCED R=
ECOVERY</strong>.</li><li>Under <strong>Select an OS</strong> image, click =
the magnifying glass and navigate to your <strong>Downloads</strong> folder=
 and select the Ubuntu ISO file.</li><li>Click <strong>Next</strong>.</li><=
li>In the <strong>Select USB drive</strong> section:<ol style=3D"list-style=
-type: lower-alpha;"><li>Select your USB drive from the list.</li><li><stro=
ng>Check the box to confirm</strong> that the USB drive will be erased and =
all data will be deleted.</li></ol></li><li>Click <strong>Next</strong>.</l=
i><li>Wait for the process to complete.</li><li>Click <strong>Finish</stron=
g> when the process is done.</li></ol><p><img src=3D"https://i.dell.com/is/=
image/DellContent/content/dam/ss2/product-images/esupport/esupport-pages/de=
ll-os-recovery-tool-advanced-another-computer.png" width=3D"600" alt=3D"" c=
lass=3D"dds__mw-100" role=3D"presentation" aria-hidden=3D"true"></p><hr><h2=
 id=3D"h_2841198707771749134489971">Installing Ubuntu on Your Dell Computer=
</h2><p>Now that you have a bootable USB drive, you can install Ubuntu on y=
our Dell computer.</p><h3>Step 1: Boot from USB Drive</h3><ol><li>Connect t=
he bootable USB drive into your computer.</li><li>Restart your computer.</l=
i><li>As soon as the Dell logo appears, press <strong>F12</strong> repeated=
ly to open the boot menu.</li><li>Use arrow keys to select your USB drive (=
usually shown as "USB Storage Device" or the drive brand name)</li><li>Pres=
s <strong>Enter</strong> to boot from the USB drive.</li></ol><h3>Step 2: S=
tart Ubuntu Installation</h3><ol><li>When the Ubuntu boot screen appears, s=
elect <strong>Dell OS Recovery</strong>.</li><li>Choose your preferred inst=
allation method and continue.</li></ol><p><img src=3D"https://i.dell.com/is=
/image/DellContent/content/dam/ss2/product-images/esupport/esupport-pages/u=
buntu-dell-recovery-erase-disk-install-ubuntu.png" width=3D"600" alt=3D"" c=
lass=3D"dds__mw-100" role=3D"presentation" aria-hidden=3D"true"></p><h3>Ste=
p 3: Complete Installation</h3><ol><li>Follow the instructions on the scree=
n to customize language, keyboard layout, time zone, and more.<strong></str=
ong></li><li>Wait for installation to complete (20-30 minutes).</li><li>Cli=
ck <strong>Restart Now</strong> when prompted.</li><li>Remove the USB drive=
 when instructed.</li><li>Press <strong>Enter</strong> to restart.</li></ol=
><p><img src=3D"https://i.dell.com/is/image/DellContent/content/dam/ss2/pro=
duct-images/esupport/esupport-pages/ubuntu-install-choose-username-password=
.png" width=3D"600" alt=3D"" class=3D"dds__mw-100" role=3D"presentation" ar=
ia-hidden=3D"true"></p><hr><h2>Post-Installation Ubuntu Setup</h2><p>After =
Ubuntu installs successfully:</p><ol><li>Update your system:<ol style=3D"li=
st-style-type: lower-alpha;"><li>Open <strong>Terminal</strong> (<strong>Ct=
rl</strong> + <strong>Alt</strong> + <strong>T</strong>)</li><li>Run <stron=
g>sudo apt update &amp;&amp; sudo apt upgrade -y</strong></li></ol></li><li=
>Configure your system:<ol style=3D"list-style-type: lower-alpha;"><li>Set =
up additional user accounts</li><li>Install your preferred software</li><li=
>Customize desktop settings</li></ol></li></ol><p><img src=3D"https://i.del=
l.com/is/image/DellContent/content/dam/ss2/product-images/esupport/esupport=
-pages/ubuntu-terminal-update-upgrade.png" width=3D"600" alt=3D"" class=3D"=
dds__mw-100" role=3D"presentation" aria-hidden=3D"true"></p><hr><h2>Frequen=
tly Asked Questions (FAQs)</h2><p>Here are some common questions that users=
 ask when creating an Ubuntu recovery media.</p><div class=3D"dds__accordio=
n__item"><h5 class=3D"dds__accordion__heading"><button id=3D"trigger-basic-=
item0" class=3D"dds__accordion__button" aria-expanded=3D"false" aria-contro=
ls=3D"content-basic-item0">  <p class=3D"accordion dds__subtitle-2" style=
=3D"">1. Can I use my Dell computer's recovery partition to install Ubuntu?=
</p><svg class=3D"dds__icon dds__accordion__icon" viewBox=3D"0 0 32 32" ari=
a-hidden=3D"true" focusable=3D"false"><path d=3D"M30.48 7.24l-14.48 14.48-1=
4.48-14.48-1.52 1.52 16 16 16-16z"></path></svg></button> </h5> <div id=3D"=
content-basic-item0" class=3D"dds__accordion__content" role=3D"region" aria=
-labelledby=3D"trigger-basic-item0"> <div class=3D"dds__accordion__body"><p=
 id=3D"Para_0"></p><ul id=3D"ul_0"><li>No, Dell recovery partitions only re=
store the original Windows operating system. You need to download the Ubunt=
u ISO separately from Dell's support website and create the installation me=
dia using the methods described above.</li></ul></div></div> </div><div cla=
ss=3D"dds__accordion__item"><h5 class=3D"dds__accordion__heading"><button i=
d=3D"trigger-basic-item1" class=3D"dds__accordion__button" aria-expanded=3D=
